The ivory tower approach is good for avoiding dead ends, I assume. However,
it seems (at least in the case of Nethack) that lack of discussion also
caused the developers to behave extremely conservatively (you could argue
that this is proof of Nethack's maturity, but see the list of balance
issues below). For example, the long-awaited 3.4.4 had things like newt's
eyes as new features; at a stage where there are some gameplay issues.

And I am not trying to deliberately bash anyone or anygame, but Nethack's
interface is years behind modern roguelikes (and I do not only mean Crawl
here). Just some examples from the top of my head (Crawl of course), many
of which have been discussed here back in the day:
* dungeon-wide searching for items
* dungeon-wide travel (Crawl started with Nethack's _ command!!)
* notes/log file
* configurable character dumps
* tutorial
* configurable hp monitors as strong as possible (I find the 'you have to
  check the hp yourself each turn' stance to be extremely anachronistic, 
  no matter what the veterans over here say)
* macros

