Okay, I give a list from my somewhat dated point of view. I will assume we
take about a spoiled, but non-too-scumming player (this is what I was and
am):
* Elbereth: this is so cheesy and powerful that I never used it in any of
  my ascensions.
* Binary resistances (this in itself is not that problematic) obtained by
  eating. First of all, this renders many monsters completely useless past
  that lunch, and it helps making all characters looking uniform (which is
  not a balance issue but annoying nonetheles).
* Wishing: too strong in that it renders most threats harmless; an
  invitation to powergaming (by going for artefacts - I never did); 
  extremely streamlined, so another reason for late game uniformness.
* Item scumming via polypiling (I usually did this, I have to confess): it
  is a quite tedious action since you'll be manually slogging tons of items
  across the dungeon but the reward is so great... If, by any chance,
  finding and wishing left open spots in your wishlist, polypiling will fill
  them.
* HP scumming (via alchemy, hence via polypiling): it is absurd to have 
  players with hp in the thousands. This is really gamebreaking. Same goes
  for mana, of course.
* AC scumming via praying: the AC game is usually interesting, until the
  lacking cap on prayer AC kills that one, too.
* Genocide: this is least problematic, but I think such strong measures 
  should have a) a (soft) cap on the number extinguished species, and b)
  some serious repercussions on the player. Currently, using genocide on
  certain monsters is a no-brainer (aside from conducts - for example, I 
  abstained from genocide since my first ascension).

Of course, one can optionally skip on any of these (challenge play). Janis,
you have a point in saying that Nethack is balanced: I believe that the
game, played without these loopholds, would be exciting and winnable (not
sure about the ascension rates, though). It may very well be that Nethack
is designed and balanced for such play. 
(Note how Spork addresses many of these issues - I think they are
universally accepted (and I didn't even mention pudding farming because it
is absurd to normal play).)
Next, while you _can_ make the game interesting with such challenges, it is
a clear design fault (IMO) to leave this to players: there are points where
the scumming approach is objectively the best one. You are forcing players
to choose a non-optimal option in order to keep it fun. (Let us not digress
into the various meanings of fun: I know that there are players out there
enjoying all the scumming.)

This is somewhat related to two other main issues everyone seems to have
with Nethack: the boredom of Gehennom (everyone used wishing, alchemy, and
all the other tricks before, so it turned into a chore) and that all
characters play the same past the midgame (same reasons actually, combined
with lack of differentiation among classes).

